# Brewfile-mac - Declarative Homebrew package management
#                macOS only: casks, Mac App Store apps, Mac-specific formulae.
#
# There is no plain `Brewfile`. Packages are split across:
#   Brewfile-core   - cross-platform
#   Brewfile-mac    - macOS only (this file)
#   Brewfile-linux  - Linux only
#
# Usage - `~/bin/bundle-sync-brewfile.sh` previews the changes, confirms,
# then installs what's missing and removes what isn't listed.
#
# VS Code extensions are not synced. `~/bin/vscode-sync-extensions.sh` is
# disabled and every `vscode` entry below is commented out. To bring it back,
# uncomment the entries you want and remove the guard at the top of that
# script - it exits early precisely because an empty list means "uninstall
# everything".
#
# Notes:
# - `brew` entries are command-line utilities.
# - `cask` entries are macOS GUI applications.
# - `mas` entries are Mac App Store applications.
# - `vscode` entries would install VS Code extensions, but nothing reads
#   them right now (see above).
# - The sync script concatenates Brewfile-core with this file. Running
#   `brew bundle` against a single file directly will see only half the
#   packages and offer to uninstall the rest.
# - The sync passes `--no-upgrade`: it installs what's missing but does not
#   upgrade what's already present. Use `brew upgrade` for that.
# - `mas` ids must match what `mas list` reports. Apple has unified some
#   iWork apps onto their former iOS ids.
# - `brew bundle dump` writes one flat file and has no idea about this
#   split, so it will clobber the layout. Add entries by hand.
#
# Warning: syncing removes anything installed but not listed here
# (except VS Code extensions, which `brew bundle` ignores).

